      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for \\( f(3) \\), we start with the given functional equation:\n\\[ f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2. \\]\n\nFirst, we need to express \\( f(3) \\) in terms of the equation. To do this, we will substitute \\( x = 3 \\) and \\( x = -2 \\) into the equation.\n\n1. Substitute \\( x = 3 \\):\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3(3)^2. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27. \\tag{1} \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( x = -2 \\):\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(1 - (-2)) = 3(-2)^2. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12. \\tag{2} \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27, \\]\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12. \\]\n\nWe can solve this system by elimination or substitution. Let's eliminate \\( f(-2) \\) by multiplying the first equation by 2 and subtracting the second equation from it:\n\\[ 2(f(3) + 2f(-2)) = 2(27), \\]\n\\[ 2f(3) + 4f(-2) = 54. \\]\nSubtracting the second equation from this result, we get:\n\\[ (2f(3) + 4f(-2)) - (f(-2) + 2f(3)) = 54 - 12, \\]\n\\[ 3f(-2) = 42, \\]\n\\[ f(-2) = 14. \\]\n\nNow substitute \\( f(-2) = 14 \\) back into the first equation:\n\\[ f(3) + 2(14) = 27, \\]\n\\[ f(3) + 28 = 27, \\]\n\\[ f(3) = 27 - 28, \\]\n\\[ f(3) = -1. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( f(3) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
